export default "# Linux Users, Groups and Authentication\n\n## Lesson Overview\n\nLinux is a multi-user operating system. This means multiple people, applications and background services can use the same computer or server while operating under different identities and permissions.\n\nFor example, imagine a cloud server hosting a marketplace application.\n\nThe server might have:\n\n- An administrator who manages the operating system.\n- A software developer who deploys application updates.\n- An application service account that runs the backend.\n- A database service account that manages database files.\n- A monitoring account that collects system information.\n\nThese identities should not all have the same level of access.\n\nLinux uses users, groups, authentication and authorization to control who can access resources and what they can do.\n\n### Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to:\n\n1. Explain how Linux users and groups work.\n2. Differentiate between normal users, root and service accounts.\n3. Create, modify and delete Linux users.\n4. Create and manage groups.\n5. Understand Linux user and group identification numbers.\n6. Explain how Linux stores account information.\n7. Understand password-based authentication.\n8. Explain SSH key authentication.\n9. Configure permissions for users and groups.\n10. Troubleshoot common authentication and access problems.\n11. Apply account security principles to a Linux server.\n\n---\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: UNDERSTANDING LINUX USERS AND GROUPS\n\n## 1. What Is a Linux User?\n\nA Linux user is an identity recognised by the operating system.\n\nWhenever someone logs into Linux or a program runs, the operating system associates that activity with a user identity.\n\nA user identity helps Linux determine:\n\n- Who is performing an action.\n- Which files the identity can access.\n- Which commands it can execute.\n- Which processes belong to it.\n- Which system resources it can use.\n\nFor example, suppose a server has three users:\n\n```text\nadmin\ndeveloper\ndatabase\n```\n\nEach user has a separate identity.\n\nThe administrator may have permission to configure the operating system, while the developer may only access application files.\n\nThe database account may only need access to database-related resources.\n\nThis separation helps prevent accidental damage and limits the impact of compromised accounts.\n\n## 2. Types of Linux Users\n\nLinux accounts can be divided into several categories.\n\n| User type | Purpose | Typical example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Root user | Full administrative control | `root` |\n| Regular user | Human interaction with the system | `developer` |\n| System user | Runs operating-system services | `www-data` |\n| Service account | Runs a particular application | `apiuser` |\n\n### 2.1 Root User\n\nThe root user is the most privileged account in Linux.\n\nIts User ID is normally:\n\n```text\nUID = 0\n```\n\nRoot can perform administrative operations such as:\n\n- Installing and removing software.\n- Creating and deleting users.\n- Modifying system configuration.\n- Changing file ownership.\n- Managing system services.\n- Configuring firewall rules.\n- Accessing protected system files.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\napt install nginx\n```\n\nInstalling a system package requires administrative privileges.\n\nA regular user normally cannot perform this operation without elevated permissions.\n\nThe administrator can use:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt install nginx\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `sudo` requests elevated privileges.\n- `apt` is the package management utility.\n- `install` tells APT to install a package.\n- `nginx` is the package being installed.\n\n**Security consideration:** Running every command as root is dangerous. A mistaken command could delete important system files or change critical configurations.\n\nUse administrative privileges only when necessary.\n\n### 2.2 Regular Users\n\nRegular users are accounts created for people who interact with the system.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nbrian\ndeveloper\noperator\nanalyst\n```\n\nA regular user usually has:\n\n- A home directory.\n- A login shell.\n- A unique UID.\n- One primary group.\n- Optional supplementary groups.\n\nA home directory normally stores personal files and user-specific configurations.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n/home/developer\n```\n\nWhen the developer logs in, Linux can use this directory as the starting location.\n\n### 2.3 System Users and Service Accounts\n\nSystem users are identities used by operating-system services and applications.\n\nConsider a web server.\n\nInstead of running its processes as root, Linux can run them under a restricted account such as:\n\n```text\nwww-data\n```\n\nThis limits what the web server can access.\n\nA custom application might use:\n\n```text\napiuser\n```\n\nThe application can run under this identity without requiring a human to log in interactively.\n\nA service account should normally receive only the permissions necessary for its application.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\napiuser\n   |\n   +---- Can read application files\n   |\n   +---- Can write application logs\n   |\n   +---- Cannot modify system configuration\n   |\n   +---- Cannot access unrelated user directories\n```\n\nThis is an application of the **principle of least privilege**.\n\nNot every system account is necessarily configured the same way. Some service accounts can log in, and others cannot. Their capabilities depend on their configuration and system policies.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Understanding UID: User Identification Number\n\nLinux does not identify users using usernames alone.\n\nEvery local user account has a numeric identifier called a User ID, or UID.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nroot       UID: 0\ndeveloper  UID: 1000\noperator   UID: 1001\napiuser    UID: 998\n```\n\nThe exact numbers depend on the distribution and account creation process.\n\nThe kernel primarily uses numeric identities when enforcing file ownership and process permissions.\n\n### Why UID Matters\n\nImagine a file belongs to:\n\n```text\nUID: 1000\nGID: 1000\n```\n\nLinux checks the identity of the process requesting access.\n\nIt can compare the process's UID and groups with the file's ownership and permissions.\n\nThis means usernames are human-readable labels, while numeric IDs are important for access control.\n\n### Viewing Your UID\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nid\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nuid=1000(developer) gid=1000(developer) groups=1000(developer),27(sudo)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Output | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `uid=1000` | Your user ID |\n| `developer` | Your username |\n| `gid=1000` | Your primary group ID |\n| `groups=` | Groups associated with your account |\n| `27(sudo)` | Membership in the sudo group |\n\nYou can also run:\n\n```bash\nid developer\n```\n\nThis displays identity information for the specified user.\n\nTo display only the UID:\n\n```bash\nid -u developer\n```\n\nTo display only the primary group ID:\n\n```bash\nid -g developer\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Understanding Linux Groups\n\nA Linux group is a collection of user identities.\n\nGroups make it easier to manage permissions for multiple users.\n\nImagine a software company has five developers who all need access to a shared application directory.\n\nInstead of granting access individually to every developer, an administrator can create a group:\n\n```text\ndevelopers\n```\n\nThen add the five users to that group.\n\nThe shared directory can be assigned to the group.\n\n### Group Structure\n\n```text\n                 Linux Server\n                      |\n             -------------------\n             |                 |\n          Users              Groups\n             |                 |\n       -------------      -------------\n       |     |     |      |           |\n      Kai   John  Mary  developers  administrators\n                         |\n                    -------------\n                    |     |     |\n                   Kai   John  Mary\n```\n\nThe diagram illustrates how several users can belong to the same group.\n\n### 4.1 Primary Group\n\nEvery Linux user has a primary group.\n\nIt is the default group associated with the user's account.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nUsername: developer\nUID: 1000\nPrimary group: developers\nGID: 1002\n```\n\nWhen the user creates a file, the file normally receives the user's primary group as its group ownership, subject to directory setgid behaviour and other filesystem rules.\n\n### 4.2 Supplementary Groups\n\nA user can belong to additional groups.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\ndeveloper\n   |\n   |---- developers\n   |\n   |---- sudo\n   |\n   |---- docker\n```\n\nThese are supplementary groups.\n\nThey provide additional access without changing the user's primary group.\n\nFor example, membership in the `sudo` group on many Ubuntu and Debian installations allows a user to request administrative privileges through sudo.\n\nHowever, group membership alone does not automatically guarantee permission to access every resource associated with a group. The relevant permissions and policies must also allow it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Understanding GID\n\nEvery Linux group has a numeric identifier called a Group ID, or GID.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndevelopers       GID: 1002\nadministrators   GID: 1003\ndatabase         GID: 1004\n```\n\nLinux uses GIDs when checking group ownership and permissions.\n\nConsider this file:\n\n```text\n-rw-r----- 1 developer developers 2048 app.log\n```\n\nIts ownership is:\n\n```text\nOwner: developer\nGroup: developers\n```\n\nThe owner can read and write.\n\nMembers of the owning group can read.\n\nOther users have no permissions through these traditional mode bits.\n\nThe actual access decision may also be affected by ACLs, security modules and other system policies.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Linux Account Information Files\n\nLinux stores local account information in several important files.\n\nUnderstanding these files is essential for system administration and security investigations.\n\n### 6.1 `/etc/passwd`\n\nThis file contains account information for local users.\n\nView it using:\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/passwd\n```\n\nExample entry:\n\n```text\ndeveloper:x:1000:1002:Software Developer:/home/developer:/bin/bash\n```\n\nEach field has a specific meaning.\n\n| Field | Value | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `developer` | Username |\n| 2 | `x` | Password information is stored elsewhere |\n| 3 | `1000` | UID |\n| 4 | `1002` | Primary GID |\n| 5 | `Software Developer` | Comment or account description |\n| 6 | `/home/developer` | Home directory |\n| 7 | `/bin/bash` | Login shell |\n\nThe fields are separated by colons.\n\nThe `x` does not represent the actual password. It indicates that password information is maintained in the shadow password database on a typical Linux system.\n\n### 6.2 `/etc/shadow`\n\nThis file stores password-related information and account password-aging fields.\n\nView it using:\n\n```bash\nsudo cat /etc/shadow\n```\n\nExample entry:\n\n```text\ndeveloper:$y$j9T$example-hash:20000:0:99999:7:::\n```\n\nThis example uses a placeholder hash.\n\nImportant fields include:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Username | Account name |\n| Password field | Password hash or special account marker |\n| Last change | Date of the last password change |\n| Minimum | Minimum number of days before another change |\n| Maximum | Maximum password age |\n| Warning | Days before expiration to warn the user |\n| Inactive | Days after expiration before account deactivation |\n| Expire | Account expiration date |\n\nThe shadow file is highly sensitive because it contains password hashes and account security information.\n\nIt should not be readable by ordinary users.\n\nCheck its permissions:\n\n```bash\nls -l /etc/shadow\n```\n\nOn many distributions, access is restricted to root and an appropriate privileged group.\n\n**Important:** Password hashes are not plaintext passwords. However, attackers who obtain them may attempt offline password guessing.\n\n### 6.3 `/etc/group`\n\nThis file stores local group information.\n\nView it using:\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/group\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndevelopers:x:1002:kai,john,mary\n```\n\nFields:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `developers` | Group name |\n| `x` | Placeholder for group password information |\n| `1002` | GID |\n| `kai,john,mary` | Supplementary group members listed here |\n\nA user's primary group may not appear in the member list because primary group membership is recorded through the user's GID in account information.\n\n### 6.4 `/etc/gshadow`\n\nThis file stores protected group administration information.\n\nIt can contain group password hashes and group administrator or member information.\n\nAccess is restricted because it contains sensitive information.\n\n### Account File Relationship\n\n```text\n                  Linux Accounts\n                        |\n          -------------------------------\n          |              |              |\n      /etc/passwd    /etc/shadow     /etc/group\n          |              |              |\n      User details   Password data   Group details\n          |              |              |\n          ---------------|--------------\n                         |\n                  /etc/gshadow\n                         |\n               Protected group data\n```\n\nThese files form part of the traditional local Linux account database.\n\nSome systems also use external identity services such as LDAP, Active Directory or SSSD. Therefore, not every account visible through Linux account tools must have a corresponding local entry in these files.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Creating Linux Users\n\nAdministrators use commands to create and configure user accounts.\n\n### Creating a User with `useradd`\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m developer\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `sudo` requests administrative privileges.\n- `useradd` creates a user account.\n- `-m` creates a home directory if needed.\n- `developer` is the username.\n\nThe resulting home directory will normally be:\n\n```text\n/home/developer\n```\n\nSet a password:\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd developer\n```\n\nLinux will prompt the administrator to enter and confirm a password.\n\n### Creating a User with a Specific Shell\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash developer\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `-m` creates the home directory.\n- `-s` specifies the login shell.\n- `/bin/bash` selects Bash.\n- `developer` identifies the new account.\n\nA shell is the program that provides a command-line environment after login.\n\n### Creating a Service Account\n\nConsider an API running on a production server.\n\nCreate a restricted account:\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin apiuser\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `-r` requests a system account.\n- `-s` selects the account's shell.\n- `/usr/sbin/nologin` prevents normal interactive login.\n- `apiuser` is the account name.\n\nThe exact `nologin` path can vary between distributions. Check it with:\n\n```bash\ncommand -v nologin\n```\n\nA service account configured with `nologin` can still run services through an appropriate service manager. It is not intended for normal interactive shell access.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Modifying User Accounts\n\nAdministrators sometimes need to change existing account settings.\n\nThe `usermod` command modifies an account.\n\n### Change a User's Login Shell\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -s /bin/bash developer\n```\n\nThis changes the login shell to Bash.\n\n### Add a User to a Supplementary Group\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `usermod` modifies the user.\n- `-a` appends a group rather than replacing existing supplementary group memberships.\n- `-G` specifies supplementary groups.\n- `developers` is the group.\n- `developer` is the account.\n\n**Important:** Forgetting `-a` when using `-G` can replace the user's existing supplementary group list. This may accidentally remove important memberships, including administrative access.\n\nVerify the result:\n\n```bash\nid developer\n```\n\n### Lock a User Account's Password\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd -l developer\n```\n\nThis locks the password authentication field.\n\nHowever, locking a password does not necessarily disable SSH key authentication or every other possible login method.\n\nFor a full account restriction, administrators must examine all authentication methods and account policies.\n\n### Unlock a User's Password\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd -u developer\n```\n\nThis unlocks the password field, assuming the account has a valid password configuration.\n\n### Change a Username\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -l newdeveloper developer\n```\n\nThis changes the login name from `developer` to `newdeveloper`.\n\nIt does not automatically rename the home directory.\n\nTo rename the home directory as part of an account change, administrators may use additional options and carefully check the account's active processes and files.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Deleting Linux Users\n\nTo delete an account:\n\n```bash\nsudo userdel developer\n```\n\nThis removes the account but normally leaves its home directory and files behind.\n\nTo remove the account and its home directory:\n\n```bash\nsudo userdel -r developer\n```\n\nThe `-r` option requests removal of the user's home directory and mail spool where applicable.\n\n**Security warning:** Deleting a user account does not necessarily remove all files owned by that UID throughout the server.\n\nFor example, files in application directories, backups or mounted storage may remain.\n\nBefore deleting an account:\n\n1. Confirm that it is no longer needed.\n2. Check whether its processes are running.\n3. Identify files owned by its UID.\n4. Review scheduled tasks and SSH access.\n5. Preserve necessary records.\n6. Remove the account carefully.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Managing Linux Groups\n\nLinux provides commands for creating, modifying and deleting groups.\n\n### Create a Group\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd developers\n```\n\nThis creates a group called `developers`.\n\n### Add a User to a Group\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer\n```\n\nThis adds `developer` to the supplementary `developers` group.\n\n### Check Group Membership\n\n```bash\ngroups developer\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndeveloper : developer developers sudo\n```\n\nThe output lists the user's group memberships.\n\n### View a Group's Information\n\n```bash\ngetent group developers\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndevelopers:x:1002:developer,kai\n```\n\n`getent` queries the system's configured account databases. This is often more useful than reading `/etc/group` directly because it can also return information from configured external identity sources.\n\n### Remove a User from a Group\n\nOn systems that provide the `gpasswd` command:\n\n```bash\nsudo gpasswd -d developer developers\n```\n\nThis removes `developer` from the supplementary group `developers`.\n\n### Delete a Group\n\n```bash\nsudo groupdel developers\n```\n\nThe group should not be a primary group for any existing user.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Linux User Permissions and Group Permissions\n\nUsers and groups become especially useful when combined with file permissions.\n\nLinux traditionally represents file access using three categories:\n\n1. Owner.\n2. Group.\n3. Others.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nls -l report.txt\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n-rw-r----- 1 developer developers 2048 report.txt\n```\n\nBreak down the permission string:\n\n```text\n- rw- r-- ---\n  |   |   |\n  |   |   +---- Others\n  |   +-------- Group\n  +------------ Owner\n```\n\nThe first character indicates the file type.\n\nFor a regular file, it is `-`.\n\nThe remaining nine characters describe permissions.\n\n| Permission | Meaning | Numeric value |\n|---|---|---:|\n| `r` | Read | 4 |\n| `w` | Write | 2 |\n| `x` | Execute | 1 |\n| `-` | No permission | 0 |\n\nThe example has:\n\n- Owner: `rw-` = read and write.\n- Group: `r--` = read only.\n- Others: `---` = no permissions.\n\n### Changing File Permissions\n\n```bash\nchmod 640 report.txt\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `chmod` changes file mode permissions.\n- `640` specifies the new permission values.\n- `report.txt` is the target file.\n\nCalculation:\n\n```text\nOwner: 4 + 2 = 6\nGroup: 4     = 4\nOthers: 0    = 0\n```\n\nTherefore:\n\n```text\n640 = rw-r-----\n```\n\n### Changing File Ownership\n\n```bash\nsudo chown developer:developers report.txt\n```\n\nThis changes:\n\n- Owner to `developer`.\n- Group to `developers`.\n\n### Shared Group Directory\n\nImagine three developers need to work on the same project.\n\nCreate a shared directory:\n\n```bash\nsudo mkdir -p /opt/company-project\n```\n\nCreate a group:\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd developers\n```\n\nAssign ownership:\n\n```bash\nsudo chown root:developers /opt/company-project\n```\n\nSet permissions:\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 2770 /opt/company-project\n```\n\nThe leading `2` sets the setgid bit on the directory.\n\nFor directories, setgid normally causes newly created entries to inherit the directory's group ownership.\n\nThe remaining `770` means:\n\n- Owner: full access.\n- Group: full access.\n- Others: no access.\n\nAdd the developers to the group:\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer\n```\n\nA new login session is normally needed before the user's supplementary group membership is reflected in that session.\n\nCheck:\n\n```bash\nid developer\n```\n\n**Important:** The setgid bit helps maintain group ownership, but it does not guarantee that every newly created file will be group-writable. The creator's umask and other permission settings also affect the final mode.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Understanding Authentication and Authorization\n\nThese two terms are fundamental to Linux security.\n\n### Authentication\n\nAuthentication answers:\n\n**Who are you?**\n\nLinux needs to verify the identity of a person or service before granting access through a particular authentication method.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Password authentication.\n- SSH public-key authentication.\n- Multi-factor authentication.\n- Biometric authentication where configured.\n- Authentication through a central identity provider.\n\n### Authorization\n\nAuthorization answers:\n\n**What are you allowed to do?**\n\nAfter identity verification, Linux and its security mechanisms determine what resources the identity can access.\n\nFor example:\n\nA developer successfully logs in to a Linux server.\n\nHowever, the developer may not have permission to read:\n\n```text\n/etc/shadow\n```\n\nThe identity has been authenticated, but access to the protected file is not authorized.\n\n### Authentication vs Authorization\n\n| Authentication | Authorization |\n|---|---|\n| Verifies identity | Determines permitted actions |\n| Answers who you are | Answers what you can access |\n| Uses passwords, keys or other credentials | Uses permissions, groups and policies |\n| Happens during identity verification | Is checked when access is requested |\n\n### Access Control Flow\n\n```text\n             User requests access\n                      |\n                      v\n              Authentication\n                      |\n             -----------------\n             |               |\n          Success           Failure\n             |               |\n             v               v\n       Identity known     Access denied\n             |\n             v\n        Authorization\n             |\n       ---------------\n       |             |\n     Allowed       Denied\n       |             |\n       v             v\n   Access given   Access refused\n```\n\nA successful login does not mean a user automatically receives administrative privileges.\n\n---\n\n## 13. What Is PAM?\n\nPAM stands for Pluggable Authentication Modules.\n\nIt is a framework used by many Linux applications and services to handle authentication-related operations.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Logging in through a terminal.\n- Using `sudo`.\n- Changing passwords.\n- Authenticating through SSH, depending on its configuration.\n\nPAM allows system administrators to configure authentication policies without rewriting each application.\n\n### How PAM Works\n\n```text\n       User enters credentials\n                  |\n                  v\n          Login application\n                  |\n                  v\n              PAM system\n                  |\n        ---------------------\n        |         |         |\n      Module    Module    Module\n        |         |         |\n      Account  Password  MFA policy\n        |\n        v\n   Authentication result\n        |\n        v\n   Access granted or denied\n```\n\nPAM modules can perform different checks.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Verify password credentials.\n- Enforce account restrictions.\n- Apply password policies.\n- Check authentication requirements.\n- Manage session setup and cleanup.\n\nPAM configuration is commonly located in:\n\n```text\n/etc/pam.d/\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\nls /etc/pam.d/\n```\n\nYou may see configuration files for `sudo`, `login`, `sshd` and other services.\n\n**Security warning:** Incorrect PAM configuration can prevent users, including administrators, from logging in. Always maintain an alternative administrative session or recovery method before changing authentication policies.\n\n---\n\n## 14. What Is NSS?\n\nNSS stands for Name Service Switch.\n\nIt determines where Linux retrieves identity and other system information.\n\nFor example, when an application requests information about a username, NSS determines which configured sources should be consulted.\n\nPossible sources include:\n\n- Local files.\n- LDAP directories.\n- DNS for certain lookup categories.\n- Other configured identity services.\n\nIts configuration is commonly found in:\n\n```text\n/etc/nsswitch.conf\n```\n\nInspect it:\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/nsswitch.conf\n```\n\nA typical user database configuration might include:\n\n```text\npasswd: files systemd\ngroup:  files systemd\nshadow: files\n```\n\nThe actual configuration varies between distributions.\n\nNSS and PAM have different responsibilities:\n\n| Component | Main responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| NSS | Locating account and identity information |\n| PAM | Providing authentication and related policy mechanisms |\n\nFor example, NSS helps locate the `developer` account, while PAM may help verify the credentials used during login.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Part 1 Knowledge Check\n\n1. What is a Linux user?\n2. What is the UID of the root account normally?\n3. Why should applications use restricted service accounts?\n4. What is the difference between a primary group and a supplementary group?\n5. What is a GID?\n6. What information is stored in `/etc/passwd`?\n7. Why is `/etc/shadow` sensitive?\n8. What does `usermod -aG` do?\n9. What is authentication?\n10. What is authorization?\n11. What is PAM?\n12. What is NSS?\n\n**Part 1 Summary**\n\nYou have learned that Linux uses users and groups to organise identities and control access. You have also examined local account databases, user-management commands, file permissions and the distinction between authentication and authorization.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: LINUX AUTHENTICATION METHODS AND SECURITY\n\n## 16. Understanding Linux Login Authentication\n\nWhen someone attempts to log into Linux, the system must establish whether the person can prove the identity they claim to have.\n\nConsider a developer logging into a remote Ubuntu server.\n\n```text\nDeveloper's computer\n        |\n        | Login request\n        v\n    Linux server\n        |\n        v\n Authentication mechanism\n        |\n        v\n Credential verification\n        |\n        v\n Account and policy checks\n        |\n        v\n Login session\n```\n\nThe exact authentication sequence depends on the service, PAM configuration and authentication method.\n\nCommon methods include:\n\n- Password authentication.\n- SSH public-key authentication.\n- Multi-factor authentication.\n- Centralised authentication.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Password Authentication\n\nPassword authentication is one of the most familiar methods.\n\nA user enters a username and password.\n\nLinux verifies the submitted password against the account's stored password verifier.\n\nA typical local password authentication process involves:\n\n1. The user supplies a username.\n2. The authentication service identifies the account.\n3. The password is processed using the configured password-hashing mechanism.\n4. The resulting verifier is checked against the stored information.\n5. Authentication succeeds or fails.\n6. Account and session policies are applied.\n\n### Password Hashing\n\nLinux systems do not normally store users' plaintext passwords in `/etc/shadow`.\n\nInstead, they store password hashes using a password-hashing scheme.\n\nA simplified representation:\n\n```text\nOriginal password\n       |\n       v\nPassword hashing algorithm\n       |\n       v\nStored password verifier\n```\n\nWhen the user logs in:\n\n```text\nSubmitted password\n       |\n       v\nPassword hashing process\n       |\n       v\nCompare with stored verifier\n       |\n       v\nAuthentication result\n```\n\nPassword hashing is designed to make recovering the original password computationally difficult.\n\nModern password storage mechanisms use salts and intentionally expensive computation.\n\nCommon password-hashing schemes include:\n\n- yescrypt.\n- bcrypt.\n- SHA-512 crypt.\n- Argon2 in systems and applications that support it.\n\nA salt is additional random data used in password hashing. It helps prevent attackers from efficiently reusing precomputed hash tables across accounts.\n\n**Important:** A password hash is not encryption. Encryption is generally reversible with the correct key, while a secure password hash is designed for verification rather than recovery.\n\n### Why Password Complexity Matters\n\nConsider these examples:\n\n```text\npassword123\n```\n\n```text\nMango!River_Cloud_72\n```\n\nThe second example is longer and less predictable.\n\nHowever, length alone is not a complete security strategy.\n\nA secure password should:\n\n- Be long.\n- Be unique to the account.\n- Avoid predictable personal information.\n- Avoid common password patterns.\n- Be stored in a password manager where possible.\n\nA unique password prevents a password leaked from one service from automatically compromising other accounts.\n\n---\n\n## 18. Password Aging and Expiration\n\nLinux provides tools for managing password-aging information.\n\nOne of these tools is:\n\n```bash\nchage\n```\n\nView password-aging information:\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -l developer\n```\n\nThis displays details such as:\n\n- Last password change.\n- Password expiration.\n- Minimum password age.\n- Maximum password age.\n- Account inactivity information.\n\n### Set Maximum Password Age\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -M 90 developer\n```\n\nThis sets the maximum password age to 90 days.\n\n### Set Minimum Password Age\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -m 1 developer\n```\n\nThis sets a minimum interval of one day before another password change.\n\n### Set a Password Expiration Date\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -E 2027-12-31 developer\n```\n\nThis sets an account expiration date.\n\n**Security consideration:** Password expiration policies should be based on organisational requirements and risk. Unnecessary frequent password changes can encourage users to choose predictable variations. Immediate password changes are particularly important after suspected compromise or credential exposure.\n\n---\n\n## 19. SSH Authentication\n\nSSH stands for Secure Shell.\n\nIt is a protocol used to securely access and administer remote systems.\n\nFor example, a software developer in Kenya can connect to a Linux server hosted in another country.\n\nA typical connection command is:\n\n```bash\nssh developer@203.0.113.10\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `ssh` starts the SSH client.\n- `developer` is the remote username.\n- `@` separates the username from the destination.\n- `203.0.113.10` represents the server address.\n\nThe address is an example documentation IP address, not a real production server.\n\nSSH can use password authentication, public-key authentication and other configured methods.\n\n---\n\n## 20. SSH Public-Key Authentication\n\nPublic-key authentication uses a cryptographic key pair.\n\nThe pair consists of:\n\n1. A private key.\n2. A public key.\n\nThe private key should remain protected on the client device.\n\nThe public key can be installed on the remote server.\n\n### Key Pair Architecture\n\n```text\n          Developer's computer\n                  |\n          --------------------\n          |                  |\n      Private key        Public key\n          |                  |\n    Kept secret         Shared with server\n                             |\n                             v\n                      Linux server\n                             |\n                             v\n                   authorized_keys file\n```\n\nThe server uses the public key to verify that the connecting client can prove possession of the corresponding private key.\n\nThe private key itself should not be copied to the server.\n\n### Generate an SSH Key Pair\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"developer-laptop\"\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `ssh-keygen` generates SSH keys.\n- `-t` specifies the key type.\n- `ed25519` selects the Ed25519 public-key algorithm.\n- `-C` adds a descriptive comment.\n- `\"developer-laptop\"` identifies the key.\n\nThe program normally creates:\n\n```text\n~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\n```\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `id_ed25519` | Private key |\n| `id_ed25519.pub` | Public key |\n\nProtect the private key.\n\nA passphrase can also be used to protect it when stored on the computer.\n\n### Copy the Public Key to the Server\n\nWhere available, use:\n\n```bash\nssh-copy-id developer@203.0.113.10\n```\n\nThis installs the public key into the remote user's authorized keys.\n\nThe relevant file is normally:\n\n```text\n~/.ssh/authorized_keys\n```\n\nThe server checks this file when public-key authentication is attempted.\n\n### Connect Using the Key\n\n```bash\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 developer@203.0.113.10\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `-i` specifies the identity file.\n- `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` is the private key path.\n- `developer` is the remote account.\n- The final value is the server address.\n\n### Common SSH Permission Problems\n\nSSH may reject a private key if its permissions are too open.\n\nCheck:\n\n```bash\nls -l ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n```\n\nA common permission setting is:\n\n```bash\nchmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n```\n\nThis grants read and write permissions to the owner only.\n\nThe `.ssh` directory commonly uses:\n\n```bash\nchmod 700 ~/.ssh\n```\n\nThe public key normally does not require the same secrecy as the private key.\n\n**Important:** SSH key authentication depends on both client and server configuration. Correct key files alone do not guarantee access if the server rejects the account, key type, permissions or authentication policy.\n\n---\n\n## 21. SSH Server Configuration\n\nThe SSH server configuration is commonly located at:\n\n```text\n/etc/ssh/sshd_config\n```\n\nView relevant settings:\n\n```bash\nsudo grep -E '^(PermitRootLogin|PasswordAuthentication|PubkeyAuthentication)' /etc/ssh/sshd_config\n```\n\nPossible settings include:\n\n```text\nPermitRootLogin no\nPasswordAuthentication no\nPubkeyAuthentication yes\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Setting | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `PermitRootLogin` | Controls direct root login |\n| `PasswordAuthentication` | Controls password authentication |\n| `PubkeyAuthentication` | Controls public-key authentication |\n\nThese are example security settings, not universal defaults.\n\nModern OpenSSH configurations may also use included files under:\n\n```text\n/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/\n```\n\nA setting in an included file may affect the final configuration.\n\n### Inspect the Effective SSH Configuration\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nsudo sshd -T\n```\n\nThis displays the effective server configuration as interpreted by the SSH daemon.\n\nYou can filter relevant settings:\n\n```bash\nsudo sshd -T | grep -E 'permitrootlogin|passwordauthentication|pubkeyauthentication'\n```\n\n### Validate SSH Configuration\n\nBefore reloading SSH after changes:\n\n```bash\nsudo sshd -t\n```\n\nThis checks the configuration syntax.\n\nIf there is no output and the command exits successfully, the syntax check passed.\n\n### Reload SSH\n\nOn many Ubuntu systems:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reload ssh\n```\n\nOther distributions may use:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reload sshd\n```\n\n**Critical remote administration warning:**\n\nNever disable password authentication before confirming that key-based access works.\n\nA safer procedure is:\n\n1. Keep your current administrative SSH session open.\n2. Install the public key.\n3. Open a second terminal.\n4. Test key-based login.\n5. Validate SSH configuration.\n6. Change the authentication policy.\n7. Reload the SSH service.\n8. Test a fresh login again.\n\nThis reduces the risk of locking yourself out of the server.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Multi-Factor Authentication\n\nMulti-factor authentication, or MFA, requires more than one category of proof to establish identity.\n\nCommon factor categories include:\n\n| Factor | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Knowledge | Something you know | Password |\n| Possession | Something you have | Security key |\n| Inherence | Something you are | Fingerprint |\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nUsername\n   |\n   v\nPassword verification\n   |\n   v\nSecond authentication factor\n   |\n   v\nAccess granted\n```\n\nA password combined with a second factor can reduce the risk of account compromise if the password is stolen.\n\n### Common MFA Methods\n\n- Time-based one-time passwords (TOTP).\n- Hardware security keys.\n- Push-based authentication.\n- Smart cards.\n- Biometrics where supported.\n\n### TOTP\n\nTOTP stands for Time-Based One-Time Password.\n\nAn authenticator application generates short-lived codes using a shared secret and the current time.\n\nA server independently calculates the expected code and verifies the submitted value.\n\nThe code changes periodically, commonly every 30 seconds.\n\nTOTP is useful, but phishing-resistant hardware security keys using appropriate authentication protocols generally provide stronger protection against credential phishing.\n\n---\n\n## 23. Sudo and Privilege Escalation\n\nLinux uses privilege elevation to allow authorised users to perform administrative tasks without constantly operating as root.\n\nThe `sudo` command is widely used for this purpose.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl restart nginx\n```\n\nThis requests elevated permission to restart the Nginx service.\n\n### Why Sudo Is Important\n\nSuppose a developer needs to restart an application service but should not have unrestricted root access.\n\nAn administrator can configure a limited sudo rule.\n\nInstead of allowing complete administrative access, the account can be permitted to execute specific commands.\n\n### Sudo Configuration\n\nSudo policy is commonly configured through:\n\n```text\n/etc/sudoers\n```\n\nAdditional rules can be placed in:\n\n```text\n/etc/sudoers.d/\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nsudo visudo\n```\n\n`visudo` checks sudoers syntax before installing the modified configuration.\n\nThis is safer than editing the file blindly.\n\n### Check Sudo Permissions\n\n```bash\nsudo -l\n```\n\nThis displays the commands the current user is permitted to execute through sudo.\n\n### Security Principle\n\nAvoid granting unnecessary administrative privileges.\n\nFor example, an application developer may need permission to restart one service but may not need permission to:\n\n- Change all system passwords.\n- Modify firewall policies.\n- Read every user's files.\n- Install arbitrary software.\n- Modify authentication configuration.\n\nSudo rules should be carefully restricted.\n\n---\n\n## 24. Account Lockout and Brute-Force Protection\n\nA brute-force attack attempts many possible passwords until one succeeds.\n\nFor example, an attacker may repeatedly attempt to access an SSH server.\n\n```text\nAttempt 1: password123\nAttempt 2: admin123\nAttempt 3: welcome123\nAttempt 4: another guess\n          ...\n```\n\nRepeated failed attempts can indicate:\n\n- Password guessing.\n- Automated scanning.\n- Credential stuffing.\n- Misconfigured applications.\n- A legitimate user repeatedly entering the wrong password.\n\n### Detect Failed Login Attempts\n\nOn Debian or Ubuntu systems, authentication messages may be found in:\n\n```bash\nsudo grep 'Failed password' /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nOn systems using systemd journals, inspect SSH service logs:\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -u ssh\n```\n\nThe service name may instead be `sshd`.\n\n### Fail2ban\n\nFail2ban is a security tool that monitors log files and applies temporary blocking rules when configured patterns of repeated failures are detected.\n\nSimplified operation:\n\n```text\nLogin attempts\n      |\n      v\nAuthentication logs\n      |\n      v\nFail2ban monitors logs\n      |\n      v\nRepeated failures detected\n      |\n      v\nConfigured firewall action\n      |\n      v\nSource address temporarily blocked\n```\n\nFail2ban is useful but should not be treated as a replacement for secure authentication.\n\nIt can also block legitimate users if configured too aggressively.\n\n---\n\n## 25. Account Security Auditing\n\nAdministrators should periodically review user accounts.\n\n### List Local User Accounts\n\n```bash\ncut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `cut` extracts selected fields.\n- `-d:` selects the colon as the delimiter.\n- `-f1` selects the first field.\n- `/etc/passwd` is the input file.\n\nThis displays usernames from the local account file.\n\nIt does not necessarily list accounts supplied by external identity services.\n\n### Identify Accounts with UID 0\n\n```bash\nawk -F: '$3 == 0 {print $1}' /etc/passwd\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `awk` processes text records.\n- `-F:` selects the colon as the field separator.\n- `$3` represents the third field, which is the UID.\n- `== 0` selects accounts with UID zero.\n- `{print $1}` prints the username.\n\nNormally, the root account should be the only account with UID zero.\n\nUnexpected UID-zero accounts require immediate investigation.\n\n### Find Users with Login Shells\n\n```bash\nawk -F: '$7 ~ /(bash|sh|zsh)$/ {print $1, $7}' /etc/passwd\n```\n\nThis searches local account entries for selected shell paths.\n\nIt is only a preliminary check: a login shell does not prove that an account is authorised, and some legitimate service accounts may have unusual configurations.\n\n### Check Inactive Accounts\n\n```bash\nlastlog\n```\n\nThis displays information about the last login for accounts where login records are available.\n\nAn account with no recent login is not automatically malicious. It may be a service account or an account created for occasional use.\n\nInvestigate accounts in context.\n\n---\n\n## 26. Part 2 Practical Exercise: Secure a Developer Account\n\nYou are administering an Ubuntu server.\n\nYour organisation has hired a developer who needs access to application files but should not have unrestricted root privileges.\n\nPerform these tasks in a test environment:\n\n**Step 1: Create the developer account**\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash developer\n```\n\n**Step 2: Set the password**\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd developer\n```\n\n**Step 3: Create a developers group**\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd developers\n```\n\nIf the group already exists, do not recreate it.\n\n**Step 4: Add the developer to the group**\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer\n```\n\n**Step 5: Create a shared directory**\n\n```bash\nsudo mkdir -p /opt/project\n```\n\n**Step 6: Assign group ownership**\n\n```bash\nsudo chown root:developers /opt/project\n```\n\n**Step 7: Configure directory permissions**\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 2770 /opt/project\n```\n\n**Step 8: Verify the account**\n\n```bash\nid developer\n```\n\n**Step 9: Verify directory permissions**\n\n```bash\nls -ld /opt/project\n```\n\nExpected permission structure:\n\n```text\ndrwxrws--- root developers /opt/project\n```\n\nThe exact output includes additional information such as ownership and modification time.\n\n**Step 10: Test access**\n\nOpen a session as the developer:\n\n```bash\nsudo -iu developer\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\ncd /opt/project\ntouch test.txt\nls -l\n```\n\nThe developer should be able to create files in the directory if the account has the intended group membership and the directory is configured correctly.\n\nExit the session:\n\n```bash\nexit\n```\n\n### Part 2 Knowledge Check\n\n1. What is password hashing?\n2. What is a salt?\n3. Why should passwords not be stored in plaintext?\n4. What does SSH stand for?\n5. What is the difference between an SSH private key and public key?\n6. What is the purpose of `authorized_keys`?\n7. What does `chmod 600` do?\n8. What is PAM?\n9. What is MFA?\n10. What is TOTP?\n11. What is sudo?\n12. Why should `visudo` be used?\n13. What is a brute-force attack?\n14. How does Fail2ban help?\n15. Why should administrators review UID-zero accounts?\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: PRACTICAL LINUX ACCOUNT MANAGEMENT AND TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nThis part combines user management, group management, permissions and authentication into realistic administration scenarios.\n\n## 27. Scenario: Managing a Software Development Team\n\nImagine you are managing a Linux server for a software company.\n\nThe team has:\n\n- Two developers.\n- One system administrator.\n- One backend API.\n- One database service.\n\nYou need to create separate identities and ensure that each account receives only the access it requires.\n\n### Proposed Account Structure\n\n| Account | Purpose | Access |\n|---|---|---|\n| `adminuser` | System administrator | Approved administrative commands |\n| `developer1` | Software developer | Shared project directory |\n| `developer2` | Software developer | Shared project directory |\n| `apiuser` | Backend service | Application-specific files |\n| `dbuser` | Database service | Database-specific files |\n\nThe group structure:\n\n```text\n                  Linux Server\n                       |\n             ----------------------\n             |                    |\n          developers           services\n             |                    |\n          --------            ---------\n          |      |            |       |\n      developer1 developer2  apiuser dbuser\n```\n\nThe diagram is conceptual. In a real system, you may create separate groups for each service rather than placing unrelated service accounts into one shared group.\n\n### Step 1: Create Groups\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd developers\n```\n\nThis creates the group used by software developers.\n\n### Step 2: Create Developer Accounts\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash developer1\nsudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash developer2\n```\n\nEach command creates an account and its home directory.\n\n### Step 3: Create Service Accounts\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin apiuser\nsudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin dbuser\n```\n\nThese accounts are intended to run services rather than provide normal interactive login access.\n\nCheck that the `nologin` path exists on your distribution.\n\n### Step 4: Add Developers to Their Group\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer1\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer2\n```\n\n### Step 5: Create the Project Directory\n\n```bash\nsudo mkdir -p /opt/company/project\n```\n\n### Step 6: Assign Group Ownership\n\n```bash\nsudo chown root:developers /opt/company/project\n```\n\n### Step 7: Set Directory Permissions\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 2770 /opt/company/project\n```\n\nThis allows the owner and group to access the directory, while denying access through traditional mode bits to others.\n\n### Step 8: Verify Everything\n\n```bash\nid developer1\nid developer2\nls -ld /opt/company/project\n```\n\nYou should confirm that:\n\n- Both developers belong to the `developers` group.\n- The project directory belongs to `root:developers`.\n- The setgid bit is enabled.\n- Other users do not have access through the traditional permission bits.\n\n---\n\n## 28. Practical Exercise: Understanding File Ownership\n\nCreate a file:\n\n```bash\nsudo touch /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nInspect it:\n\n```bash\nls -l /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nIt may show:\n\n```text\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 config.txt\n```\n\nThe file belongs to root because root created it.\n\nNow change ownership:\n\n```bash\nsudo chown developer1:developers /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nSet permissions:\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 640 /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nExpected permission structure:\n\n```text\n-rw-r----- developer1 developers config.txt\n```\n\nThis means:\n\n- `developer1` can read and write.\n- Members of `developers` can read.\n- Other users have no access through these mode bits.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nSuppose the configuration file contains application information.\n\nYou may want developers to read the file but prevent unrelated users from accessing it.\n\nThe permissions enforce this basic access-control requirement.\n\nHowever, sensitive credentials should receive stricter protection and should preferably be stored in a suitable secrets-management system rather than ordinary application configuration files.\n\n---\n\n## 29. Understanding Umask\n\nThe umask determines which permission bits are removed from the default permissions when new files and directories are created.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\numask\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\n0022\n```\n\nA common calculation uses:\n\n- Default regular file permissions: `666`.\n- Default directory permissions: `777`.\n\nWith a umask of `022`:\n\n```text\nRegular file: 666 with 022 = 644\nDirectory:    777 with 022 = 755\n```\n\nThis is a simplified permission-bit calculation.\n\n### Understanding the Result\n\nA new file might receive:\n\n```text\n-rw-r--r--\n```\n\nA new directory might receive:\n\n```text\ndrwxr-xr-x\n```\n\nThe owner receives more permissions than other users.\n\n### Change Umask Temporarily\n\n```bash\numask 027\n```\n\nThis affects newly created files and directories in the current shell and its child processes.\n\nExpected default modes under this umask are commonly:\n\n```text\nFile:      640\nDirectory: 750\n```\n\nThe application or program creating a file can request different modes, and umask removes bits from the requested mode.\n\n**Important:** Umask is not a complete security policy. Existing files are not automatically changed when the umask changes.\n\n---\n\n## 30. Access Control Lists (ACLs)\n\nTraditional Linux permissions provide owner, group and others categories.\n\nSometimes these are not enough.\n\nImagine a project directory belongs to the `developers` group, but one auditor also needs read-only access.\n\nYou do not necessarily want to change the directory's primary group or grant access to all other users.\n\nACLs provide more detailed access rules.\n\nACL stands for Access Control List.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nOwner: developer1\nGroup: developers\n```\n\nYou want to grant `auditor` read access to a file.\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nsudo setfacl -m u:auditor:r report.txt\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `setfacl` manages access control lists.\n- `-m` modifies an ACL.\n- `u:auditor:r` grants user `auditor` read permission.\n- `report.txt` is the target file.\n\nInspect ACLs:\n\n```bash\ngetfacl report.txt\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\n# file: report.txt\n# owner: developer1\n# group: developers\nuser::rw-\nuser:auditor:r--\ngroup::r--\nmask::r--\nother::---\n```\n\nThe ACL mask limits the effective permissions of named users and groups and the owning group class.\n\nThis is why the effective access shown by `getfacl` can differ from the permissions written in an individual ACL entry.\n\n### Remove an ACL Entry\n\n```bash\nsudo setfacl -x u:auditor report.txt\n```\n\nThis removes the named user entry.\n\nACLs are useful in shared systems where multiple users need different levels of access.\n\n---\n\n## 31. Troubleshooting Linux Login Failures\n\nA user reports:\n\n> I cannot log into the Linux server.\n\nDo not immediately reset the password.\n\nFirst, identify the cause.\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n1. Incorrect password.\n2. Account locked.\n3. Account expired.\n4. Invalid login shell.\n5. SSH authentication disabled.\n6. Missing public key.\n7. Incorrect SSH file permissions.\n8. User not permitted by SSH policy.\n9. Network connectivity problems.\n10. PAM authentication restrictions.\n\n### Step 1: Check Whether the Account Exists\n\n```bash\ngetent passwd developer\n```\n\nIf the account is available through the configured identity databases, this command should return its account information.\n\n### Step 2: Check Account Status\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd -S developer\n```\n\nThis reports password status information.\n\nThe exact output format can differ between distributions.\n\n### Step 3: Check Password Expiration\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -l developer\n```\n\nThis displays account and password-aging information.\n\n### Step 4: Check the Login Shell\n\n```bash\ngetent passwd developer\n```\n\nLook at the final field.\n\nFor a normal interactive account, it might be:\n\n```text\n/bin/bash\n```\n\nIf the shell is:\n\n```text\n/usr/sbin/nologin\n```\n\nthe account is not intended for normal interactive login.\n\n### Step 5: Inspect Authentication Logs\n\nOn Ubuntu or Debian systems:\n\n```bash\nsudo tail -n 50 /var/log/auth.log\n```\n\nOn systems using systemd journals:\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -u ssh -n 50\n```\n\nReplace `ssh` with `sshd` where appropriate.\n\nLogs may indicate whether the failure came from a bad password, rejected key, account policy or another problem.\n\n### Step 6: Check SSH Server Configuration\n\n```bash\nsudo sshd -T\n```\n\nInspect the effective authentication configuration.\n\n### Step 7: Check the Network\n\nFrom the client:\n\n```bash\nnc -vz 203.0.113.10 22\n```\n\nThis checks whether a TCP connection to port 22 can be established.\n\nIf the connection fails, investigate:\n\n- Server availability.\n- Firewall rules.\n- Routing.\n- Network security groups.\n- SSH service status.\n- Whether SSH is listening on another port.\n\nDo not assume every SSH problem is caused by a password.\n\n---\n\n## 32. Troubleshooting Permission Denied Errors\n\nImagine a developer runs:\n\n```bash\ncat /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nLinux returns:\n\n```text\nPermission denied\n```\n\nThis means the access request was denied.\n\nPossible causes:\n\n- Incorrect ownership.\n- Missing read permission.\n- Missing directory traversal permission.\n- An ACL restriction or mask.\n- Mandatory access control policy.\n- Filesystem mount restrictions.\n- The application is running under an unexpected UID or GID.\n\n### Step 1: Inspect File Permissions\n\n```bash\nls -l /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\n### Step 2: Inspect Directory Permissions\n\n```bash\nls -ld /opt\nls -ld /opt/company\nls -ld /opt/company/project\n```\n\nWhy check the directories?\n\nLinux needs execute permission on each directory in a path to traverse it.\n\nA user may have read permission on a file but still be unable to reach it because a parent directory does not permit traversal.\n\n### Step 3: Inspect ACLs\n\n```bash\ngetfacl /opt/company/project/config.txt\n```\n\nThis reveals additional access-control rules.\n\n### Step 4: Inspect the Current Identity\n\n```bash\nid\n```\n\nThe current shell may not contain a newly added supplementary group.\n\nA new login session may be needed.\n\n### Step 5: Test Access Carefully\n\n```bash\ntest -r /opt/company/project/config.txt && echo \"Readable\" || echo \"Not readable\"\n```\n\nThis checks whether the current identity can read the file.\n\nThe same approach can be used with `-w` for write access and `-x` for execute or directory traversal access.\n\n---\n\n## 33. Understanding Process Identity\n\nLinux does not only apply user and group identities to interactive logins.\n\nProcesses also operate with security credentials.\n\nFor example, a Node.js API may run as:\n\n```text\napiuser\n```\n\nInspect running processes:\n\n```bash\nps aux\n```\n\nA more focused command:\n\n```bash\nps -eo user,group,pid,cmd\n```\n\nThis displays:\n\n- User.\n- Group.\n- Process ID.\n- Command.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nUSER       GROUP       PID  COMMAND\nroot       root          1  /sbin/init\napiuser    apiuser    1520  node server.js\n```\n\nThe API process operates with the identity and privileges assigned to `apiuser`.\n\nThis is important because a compromised application process should not automatically receive root-level access.\n\n### Why Process Identity Matters\n\nSuppose an attacker exploits a vulnerability in a Node.js API.\n\nIf the API runs as root, the attacker may gain access to many system resources.\n\nIf the API runs under a restricted service account, the impact may be limited by:\n\n- File permissions.\n- Group membership.\n- Service sandboxing.\n- Mandatory access control.\n- Network restrictions.\n- Other system security controls.\n\nRunning services as non-root is an important security practice, but it does not by itself guarantee that an application is secure.\n\n---\n\n## 34. Understanding `su` and `sudo`\n\nLinux provides different ways to operate under another identity.\n\n### `su`\n\nThe `su` command switches to another user account.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsu - developer\n```\n\nThis starts a login shell for `developer`, usually requiring that account's password unless the caller is appropriately privileged.\n\n### `sudo`\n\nThe `sudo` command executes an approved command with elevated or otherwise configured privileges.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nsudo -u apiuser whoami\n```\n\nThis runs `whoami` as `apiuser`.\n\nExpected output:\n\n```text\napiuser\n```\n\n### Difference\n\n| `su` | `sudo` |\n|---|---|\n| Switches user identity | Executes commands under an approved identity |\n| Often opens another shell | Usually executes a specified command |\n| Commonly authenticates using target account credentials | Commonly authenticates using the caller's credentials |\n| Behaviour depends on configuration | Behaviour depends on sudoers policy |\n\nThe `-` option with `su` requests a login-style environment.\n\n---\n\n## 35. Practical Project: Secure a Linux Application Server\n\n### Project Scenario\n\nYou have deployed a backend API to an Ubuntu server.\n\nYou need to establish secure user accounts and protect the application directory.\n\n### Requirements\n\nCreate:\n\n- A developer account.\n- A developer group.\n- An application service account.\n- A protected application directory.\n\n### Step 1: Create the Group\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd appdevelopers\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create the Developer\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash developer\n```\n\nSet the password:\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd developer\n```\n\n### Step 3: Add Developer to the Group\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG appdevelopers developer\n```\n\n### Step 4: Create the Application Account\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin apiuser\n```\n\n### Step 5: Create the Application Directory\n\n```bash\nsudo mkdir -p /opt/marketplace\n```\n\n### Step 6: Assign Ownership\n\n```bash\nsudo chown apiuser:appdevelopers /opt/marketplace\n```\n\n### Step 7: Set Permissions\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 2750 /opt/marketplace\n```\n\nThis grants:\n\n- Owner: full access.\n- Group: read and directory traversal.\n- Others: no access through traditional mode bits.\n- Setgid: new entries inherit the directory's group ownership.\n\n### Step 8: Verify\n\n```bash\nls -ld /opt/marketplace\n```\n\nExpected permission structure:\n\n```text\ndrwxr-s--- apiuser appdevelopers /opt/marketplace\n```\n\n### Step 9: Inspect the Service Identity\n\n```bash\nid apiuser\n```\n\n### Step 10: Test a Command as the Service User\n\n```bash\nsudo -u apiuser whoami\n```\n\nExpected output:\n\n```text\napiuser\n```\n\n### Step 11: Review Account Configuration\n\n```bash\ngetent passwd developer\ngetent passwd apiuser\ngetent group appdevelopers\n```\n\n### Project Security Questions\n\n1. Why should the API not run as root?\n2. Why does the developer belong to a separate group?\n3. Why is the service account configured with `nologin`?\n4. Why does the application directory use restricted permissions?\n5. What could happen if the application directory were writable by everyone?\n6. Why is it important to inspect process identities?\n\n### Part 3 Summary\n\nYou have practised managing a software team on Linux, setting up group-based access, understanding umask and ACLs, troubleshooting login failures, investigating permission problems and controlling application process identities.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT, QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nThis section evaluates your understanding of Linux users, groups and authentication.\n\nEach question includes its correct answer and an explanation.\n\n## 36. Conceptual Assessment\n\n### Q1. What is a Linux user?\n\n**Answer:** A Linux user is an identity recognised by the operating system.\n\n**Explanation:** Linux associates users with numeric identifiers and uses those identities when controlling access to files, processes and system resources.\n\n### Q2. What is the UID of the root account normally?\n\n**Answer:** `0`.\n\n**Explanation:** UID 0 represents the root identity, which has extensive administrative privileges.\n\n### Q3. What is the purpose of a service account?\n\n**Answer:** To run a particular application or service under a separate, restricted identity.\n\n**Explanation:** A web application should not need unrestricted root access to serve requests. A dedicated service account helps limit its permissions.\n\n### Q4. What is a GID?\n\n**Answer:** Group Identification Number.\n\n**Explanation:** It is the numeric identifier Linux associates with a group.\n\n### Q5. What is the difference between a primary and supplementary group?\n\n**Answer:** A primary group is the default group associated with a user, while supplementary groups provide additional group memberships.\n\n**Explanation:** Supplementary groups allow a user to access resources shared with multiple groups.\n\n### Q6. What is stored in `/etc/passwd`?\n\n**Answer:** Local account information such as usernames, UIDs, GIDs, home directories and login shells.\n\n**Explanation:** It provides the traditional local user account database.\n\n### Q7. Why is `/etc/shadow` protected?\n\n**Answer:** It contains sensitive password-related information, including password hashes.\n\n**Explanation:** Attackers who obtain password hashes may attempt offline password guessing.\n\n### Q8. What is authentication?\n\n**Answer:** The process of verifying an identity.\n\n**Explanation:** A password or SSH private-key proof can be used to establish that a person or client is authorised to claim a particular identity.\n\n### Q9. What is authorization?\n\n**Answer:** Determining what an authenticated identity is permitted to access or do.\n\n**Explanation:** A user may log in successfully but still be denied access to a protected file.\n\n### Q10. What is PAM?\n\n**Answer:** Pluggable Authentication Modules.\n\n**Explanation:** PAM provides a configurable framework for authentication and related account and session policies.\n\n---\n\n## 37. Command-Based Assessment\n\n### Q11. Which command displays your current user and group IDs?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nid\n```\n\n**Explanation:** It displays the current UID, primary GID and supplementary group memberships.\n\n### Q12. Which command creates a user with a home directory?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo useradd -m developer\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The `-m` option requests creation of the home directory.\n\n### Q13. Which command sets a user's password?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo passwd developer\n```\n\n**Explanation:** The command allows an administrator to configure the account password.\n\n### Q14. What does this command do?\n\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG developers developer\n```\n\n**Answer:** It adds `developer` to the supplementary group `developers`.\n\n**Explanation:** `-aG` appends the group membership rather than replacing the existing supplementary group list.\n\n### Q15. Which command creates a group?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo groupadd developers\n```\n\n### Q16. What does this command do?\n\n```bash\nsudo chmod 640 report.txt\n```\n\n**Answer:** It gives the owner read/write permission, the group read permission and others no permission.\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n```text\n6 = 4 + 2 = rw-\n4 = r--\n0 = ---\n```\n\n### Q17. Which command changes file ownership?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo chown developer:developers report.txt\n```\n\n**Explanation:** It assigns the specified user and group as the file's owner and group.\n\n### Q18. Which command displays group memberships?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\ngroups developer\n```\n\n### Q19. Which command displays a user's password-aging information?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo chage -l developer\n```\n\n### Q20. Which command checks a user's sudo permissions?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n```bash\nsudo -l\n```\n\n**Explanation:** It lists commands the current user is allowed to execute through sudo.\n\n---\n\n## 38. Authentication and SSH Assessment\n\n### Q21. What is SSH?\n\n**Answer:** Secure Shell.\n\n**Explanation:** It provides an encrypted communication protocol for remote system access and administration.\n\n### Q22. What is the difference between an SSH private key and public key?\n\n**Answer:** The private key is kept secret by the client, while the public key is installed or shared with the server.\n\n**Explanation:** The server uses the public key to verify proof that the client possesses the corresponding private key.\n\n### Q23. What does this command do?\n\n```bash\nssh-keygen -t ed25519\n```\n\n**Answer:** It generates an Ed25519 SSH key pair.\n\n### Q24. What is the purpose of `authorized_keys`?\n\n**Answer:** It stores public keys that are permitted to authenticate as the associated account.\n\n### Q25. Why is MFA useful?\n\n**Answer:** It requires additional authentication evidence beyond a single factor, reducing the risk of account compromise when one credential is stolen.\n\n### Q26. What is a brute-force attack?\n\n**Answer:** An attack that repeatedly attempts possible credentials until a valid one is discovered.\n\n### Q27. What is Fail2ban?\n\n**Answer:** A security tool that monitors configured logs and can temporarily block sources associated with repeated suspicious activity.\n\n### Q28. Why should you not immediately disable SSH password authentication?\n\n**Answer:** You could lose access if key-based authentication is not correctly configured.\n\n**Explanation:** First install and test the public key in a separate session, then change the server policy.\n\n---\n\n## 39. Troubleshooting Assessment\n\n### Q29. A developer receives `Permission denied` when accessing a file. Name three possible causes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. The user lacks the required file permission.\n2. A parent directory does not permit traversal.\n3. An ACL or mandatory access control policy restricts access.\n\n**Explanation:** Linux checks access across the full path and may apply additional security policies beyond basic file mode bits.\n\n### Q30. A user belongs to a newly assigned group but cannot access its shared directory. What should you investigate?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n- Whether the group membership was correctly applied.\n- Whether the current login session has refreshed its supplementary groups.\n- Whether the directory's group ownership is correct.\n- Whether its group permissions allow the required action.\n- Whether ACLs or other security controls affect access.\n\n**Explanation:** A group membership change may not be reflected in an existing session.\n\n### Q31. An API is running as root. What is the security concern?\n\n**Answer:** If the API is compromised, the attacker may gain extensive system privileges.\n\n**Explanation:** Running the API under a restricted service account helps limit the potential impact.\n\n### Q32. Why is `usermod -aG` generally safer than `usermod -G` when adding one supplementary group?\n\n**Answer:** Because `-aG` appends the new group instead of replacing the existing supplementary group list.\n\n### Q33. A server rejects an SSH key even though the public key was installed. What should you investigate?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n- Whether the correct public key was installed.\n- Whether the client is using the corresponding private key.\n- Private key and server-side directory permissions.\n- Ownership of the `.ssh` directory and `authorized_keys`.\n- Effective SSH configuration.\n- Server authentication logs.\n- Account restrictions.\n\n**Explanation:** Successful key authentication depends on the key pair, file access, server configuration and account policies.\n\n### Q34. Why should you not automatically treat every account without a recent login as malicious?\n\n**Answer:** Some legitimate service accounts are not intended for interactive logins.\n\n**Explanation:** Account activity must be interpreted in the context of the system's purpose and expected behaviour.\n\n### Q35. What is the difference between NSS and PAM?\n\n**Answer:** NSS determines where system identity information is retrieved, while PAM provides configurable authentication and related policy mechanisms.\n\n---\n\n# 40. FINAL PRACTICAL PROJECT: LINUX IDENTITY AND ACCESS MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\n\n## Project Title\n\n**Design and Implement Secure Linux User, Group and Authentication Management for a Software Company**\n\n### Scenario\n\nA software company operates a Linux server that hosts its marketplace backend.\n\nThe company has:\n\n- Two software developers.\n- One system administrator.\n- One backend API service.\n- One database service.\n\nYou have been assigned responsibility for implementing the Linux identity and access management structure.\n\nYour goal is to ensure that every account has an appropriate identity and only the permissions required for its responsibilities.\n\n## Project Requirements\n\n### Task 1: Create User Groups\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\ndevelopers\nsystemadmins\n```\n\n### Task 2: Create User Accounts\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\ndeveloper1\ndeveloper2\nadminuser\n```\n\nEnsure that each human account has an appropriate home directory and login shell.\n\n### Task 3: Create Service Accounts\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\napiuser\ndbuser\n```\n\nConfigure the accounts as restricted service identities that do not permit normal interactive login.\n\n### Task 4: Assign Group Memberships\n\nAssign:\n\n```text\ndeveloper1 → developers\ndeveloper2 → developers\nadminuser  → systemadmins\n```\n\nConfigure administrative privileges only through an appropriate, carefully reviewed sudo policy.\n\n### Task 5: Create Application Directories\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\n/opt/marketplace\n/opt/marketplace/logs\n/opt/marketplace/config\n```\n\n### Task 6: Configure Ownership\n\nChoose suitable ownership for each directory.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Directory | Suggested owner | Suggested group |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/opt/marketplace` | `apiuser` | `developers` |\n| `/opt/marketplace/logs` | `apiuser` | `apiuser` |\n| `/opt/marketplace/config` | `root` | `apiuser` |\n\nExplain why each ownership choice is appropriate.\n\n### Task 7: Configure Permissions\n\nApply permissions that:\n\n- Allow authorised developers to access project files.\n- Allow the API to write application logs.\n- Prevent unrelated users from reading sensitive configuration.\n- Prevent ordinary users from changing system-owned configuration.\n\nDo not assume that one permission value is appropriate for every directory.\n\n### Task 8: Verify Account Information\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nid developer1\nid developer2\nid adminuser\nid apiuser\nid dbuser\n```\n\nAlso inspect:\n\n```bash\ngetent group developers\ngetent group systemadmins\n```\n\n### Task 9: Verify File Access\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\nls -ld /opt/marketplace\nls -ld /opt/marketplace/logs\nls -ld /opt/marketplace/config\n```\n\nExplain the owner, group and permissions shown.\n\n### Task 10: Test Process Identity\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nsudo -u apiuser whoami\n```\n\nExpected output:\n\n```text\napiuser\n```\n\nExplain why running the API under this account is safer than running it as root.\n\n### Task 11: Review Authentication Security\n\nExplain how you would configure:\n\n- Strong passwords.\n- SSH public-key authentication.\n- MFA where supported.\n- Restricted root login.\n- Secure sudo policies.\n- Authentication log monitoring.\n- Account expiration and removal procedures.\n\nDo not disable a working remote authentication method before testing its replacement.\n\n### Task 12: Produce a Security Report\n\nYour report must contain:\n\n1. The user account structure.\n2. The group structure.\n3. UID and GID explanations.\n4. Directory ownership decisions.\n5. Permission explanations.\n6. Service account restrictions.\n7. Authentication methods.\n8. Potential security weaknesses.\n9. Recommended improvements.\n10. Verification results.\n\n---\n\n# 41. LESSON COMPLETION CHECKLIST\n\nBefore submitting your work to Kai, confirm that you can:\n\n| Skill | Expected outcome |\n|---|---|\n| User management | Create, modify and delete accounts |\n| Group management | Create groups and assign users |\n| UID and GID | Explain numeric identities |\n| Account files | Explain `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/shadow` and `/etc/group` |\n| Permissions | Interpret and configure file permissions |\n| Ownership | Change file and directory ownership |\n| Authentication | Explain passwords and SSH keys |\n| Authorization | Explain access-control decisions |\n| PAM | Explain its authentication role |\n| NSS | Explain its identity lookup role |\n| Sudo | Manage administrative access safely |\n| MFA | Explain multi-factor authentication |\n| Auditing | Inspect accounts and authentication logs |\n| Troubleshooting | Diagnose login and permission problems |\n| Application security | Run services using restricted identities |\n\n---\n\n# 42. SUBMIT TO KAI FOR ASSESSMENT\n\nWhen you finish the practical project, submit your results to Kai, your Firebox AI Instructor.\n\nKai should evaluate:\n\n- Whether the user accounts were created correctly.\n- Whether group membership is appropriate.\n- Whether service accounts are restricted.\n- Whether permissions follow least privilege.\n- Whether SSH and password authentication are configured securely.\n- Whether the project directories are protected.\n- Whether the security report explains the decisions correctly.\n\n**Completion Standard:** You should be able to manage Linux identities, configure group-based access, understand authentication mechanisms and investigate common account-security problems without relying on memorised commands alone.\n\n## Lesson Complete\n\n**Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete**\n\n### Recommended Next Lesson\n\n`Linux User and Access Control: Permissions, ACLs and Privilege Management`\n\nThis lesson will build on the concepts covered here and explore Linux access control in greater depth.";
